WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.000
Gradient descent.

2
00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:04.536
Backprop tells us which way is uphill.

3
00:00:04.536 --> 00:00:06.067
Now we step the other way.

4
00:00:07.267 --> 00:00:10.533
The gradient points in the direction of steepest increase.

5
00:00:10.833 --> 00:00:14.667
So we step the opposite way, and repeat until the loss stops falling.

6
00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:17.233
Here is the update rule.

7
00:00:17.533 --> 00:00:19.500
Every weight is replaced by itself...

8
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.233
minus the learning rate, which scales the step...

9
00:00:22.533 --> 00:00:23.602
times the gradient.

10
00:00:23.602 --> 00:00:27.033
The learning rate matters more than almost any other setting.

11
00:00:28.233 --> 00:00:29.767
Picture the loss as a valley.

12
00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:33.833
With a good learning rate, each step takes us smoothly toward the bottom.

13
00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:36.600
Now the two failure modes.

14
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:40.900
Too large, and you leap across the valley and land higher than you started.

15
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.900
The loss oscillates, and can diverge to NaN.

16
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:45.787
Too small, and you creep.

17
00:00:45.787 --> 00:00:50.800
The fix is to cut a rate that blows up by ten times, and raise one that crawls.

18
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.033
Let's test three rates on a simple bowl.

19
00:00:54.333 --> 00:00:57.800
The loss is just the weight squared, with its minimum at zero.

20
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:01.100
Each step subtracts the learning rate times the gradient.

21
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:06.167
Ten diverges, point one converges, and one in ten thousand barely moves.

22
00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:09.133
Here's how it bites in practice.

23
00:01:09.433 --> 00:01:16.300
Your satellite telemetry autoencoder trains fine for two epochs, then the loss becomes NaN at three in the morning.

24
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:22.033
A burst of spikes produced big gradients, and the learning rate turned them into a catastrophic leap.

25
00:01:22.333 --> 00:01:30.500
The fix is a pair: lower the rate to one e minus three, and clip the gradient norm so no batch can throw the weights across the valley.

26
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:32.490
Let's recap.

27
00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:34.267
Step opposite the gradient.

28
00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:36.400
The learning rate scales every step.

29
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:38.900
Too big diverges, too small crawls.

30
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.566
And clip gradients as a safety net.

31
00:01:41.566 --> 00:01:44.067
Next up: stochastic gradient descent.
